[2026-02-04T17:33:24.445Z] [STDOUT] {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mm6HpRRonKVRLEMcQdh8yF","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"AI washing\" layoffs 2026 companies claim AI excuse financial constraints\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"AI layoffs or ‘AI-washing’? | TechCrunch\",\"url\":\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/01/ai-layoffs-or-ai-washing/\"},{\"title\":\"AI layoffs or ‘AI-washing’?\",\"url\":\"https://finance.yahoo.com/news/ai-layoffs-ai-washing-221553425.html\"},{\"title\":\"More companies are pointing to AI as they lay off employees - CBS News\",\"url\":\"https://www.cbsnews.com/news/ai-layoffs-2026-artificial-intelligence-amazon-pinterest/\"},{\"title\":\"AI-Washing Exposed: Are 50K+ Layoffs Really About Automation?\",\"url\":\"https://www.techbuzz.ai/articles/ai-washing-exposed-are-50k-layoffs-really-about-automation\"},{\"title\":\"AI Layoff News Sparks 'AI Washing' Worries | PYMNTS.com\",\"url\":\"https://www.pymnts.com/artificial-intelligence-2/2026/ai-layoff-news-sparks-ai-washing-worries/\"},{\"title\":\"Companies Are Laying Off Workers Because of AI’s Potential—Not Its Performance\",\"url\":\"https://hbr.org/2026/01/companies-are-laying-off-workers-because-of-ais-potential-not-its-performance\"},{\"title\":\"AI-washing in 2026 Layoffs Raises Transparency Concerns | Ukraine news - #Mezha\",\"url\":\"https://mezha.net/eng/bukvy/ai-washing-in-2026-layoffs-raises-transparency-concerns/\"},{\"title\":\"Some companies tie AI to layoffs, but the reality is more complicated\",\"url\":\"https://www.bostonherald.com/2026/02/02/did-ai-really-drive-layoffs/\"},{\"title\":\"Is AI Really To Blame For All The Tech Layoffs? - BGR\",\"url\":\"https://www.bgr.com/2086955/tech-layoffs-blame-ai-reason/\"},{\"title\":\"Some companies link AI to layoffs, but the reality is more complicated | Washington Times\",\"url\":\"https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2026/feb/2/companies-link-ai-layoffs-reality-complicated/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about \"AI-washing\" and 2026 layoffs:\n\n## The AI-Washing Trend\n\nCompanies are citing AI as the reason for layoffs that might actually be caused by other factors, like over-hiring during the pandemic, in a trend called \"AI-washing.\"\n\n## Scale of AI-Related Layoffs\n\nAI was the stated reason for more than 50,000 layoffs in 2025, with Amazon and Pinterest among the tech companies who blamed the technology for recent cuts.\n\n## The Skepticism\n\nHowever, there's growing skepticism about these claims. A Forrester report published in January argued that many companies announcing AI-related layoffs do not have mature, vetted AI applications ready to fill those roles, highlighting a trend of 'AI-washing' — attributing financially motivated cuts to future AI.\n\nCompanies are saying that they're anticipating introducing AI that will take over these jobs, but it hasn't happened yet, according to a professor at the Wharton School.\n\n## Why Companies Use This Excuse\n\nAccording to a senior research fellow at the Brookings Institute, it's a \"very investor-friendly message,\" as the alternative - admitting \"the business is ailing\" - doesn't play nearly as well in earnings calls or press releases.\n\n## Economic Research Questions the Reality\n\nA director of global macro research noted that while some jobs are potentially exposed to AI, most employers don't appear to be replacing a significant number of workers with AI. He also suggested that companies could be using AI as a pretext for job cuts, with some firms trying to \"dress up layoffs as a good news story rather than a bad one — for example, by pointing to technological change instead of past overhiring.\"\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ecff7720-544d-482b-91e9-9eb1a0db0dee","uuid":"340aaadd-45a8-4f71-baaf-f90e726462d0","tool_use_result":{"query":"\"AI washing\" layoffs 2026 companies claim AI excuse financial constraints","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01RKkA2LdP32iKUaE83foPi7","content":[{"title":"AI layoffs or ‘AI-washing’? | TechCrunch","url":"https://techcrunch.com/2026/02/01/ai-layoffs-or-ai-washing/"},{"title":"AI layoffs or ‘AI-washing’?","url":"https://finance.yahoo.com/news/ai-layoffs-ai-washing-221553425.html"},{"title":"More companies are pointing to AI as they lay off employees - CBS News","url":"https://www.cbsnews.com/news/ai-layoffs-2026-artificial-intelligence-amazon-pinterest/"},{"title":"AI-Washing Exposed: Are 50K+ Layoffs Really About Automation?","url":"https://www.techbuzz.ai/articles/ai-washing-exposed-are-50k-layoffs-really-about-automation"},{"title":"AI Layoff News Sparks 'AI Washing' Worries | PYMNTS.com","url":"https://www.pymnts.com/artificial-intelligence-2/2026/ai-layoff-news-sparks-ai-washing-worries/"},{"title":"Companies Are Laying Off Workers Because of AI’s Potential—Not Its Performance","url":"https://hbr.org/2026/01/companies-are-laying-off-workers-because-of-ais-potential-not-its-performance"},{"title":"AI-washing in 2026 Layoffs Raises Transparency Concerns | Ukraine news - #Mezha","url":"https://mezha.net/eng/bukvy/ai-washing-in-2026-layoffs-raises-transparency-concerns/"},{"title":"Some companies tie AI to layoffs, but the reality is more complicated","url":"https://www.bostonherald.com/2026/02/02/did-ai-really-drive-layoffs/"},{"title":"Is AI Really To Blame For All The Tech Layoffs? - BGR","url":"https://www.bgr.com/2086955/tech-layoffs-blame-ai-reason/"},{"title":"Some companies link AI to layoffs, but the reality is more complicated | Washington Times","url":"https://www.washingtontimes.com/news/2026/feb/2/companies-link-ai-layoffs-reality-complicated/"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about \"AI-washing\" and 2026 layoffs:\n\n## The AI-Washing Trend\n\nCompanies are citing AI as the reason for layoffs that might actually be caused by other factors, like over-hiring during the pandemic, in a trend called \"AI-washing.\"\n\n## Scale of AI-Related Layoffs\n\nAI was the stated reason for more than 50,000 layoffs in 2025, with Amazon and Pinterest among the tech companies who blamed the technology for recent cuts.\n\n## The Skepticism\n\nHowever, there's growing skepticism about these claims. A Forrester report published in January argued that many companies announcing AI-related layoffs do not have mature, vetted AI applications ready to fill those roles, highlighting a trend of 'AI-washing' — attributing financially motivated cuts to future AI.\n\nCompanies are saying that they're anticipating introducing AI that will take over these jobs, but it hasn't happened yet, according to a professor at the Wharton School.\n\n## Why Companies Use This Excuse\n\nAccording to a senior research fellow at the Brookings Institute, it's a \"very investor-friendly message,\" as the alternative - admitting \"the business is ailing\" - doesn't play nearly as well in earnings calls or press releases.\n\n## Economic Research Questions the Reality\n\nA director of global macro research noted that while some jobs are potentially exposed to AI, most employers don't appear to be replacing a significant number of workers with AI. He also suggested that companies could be using AI as a pretext for job cuts, with some firms trying to \"dress up layoffs as a good news story rather than a bad one — for example, by pointing to technological change instead of past overhiring.\""],"durationSeconds":8.995717999999993}}
